\section{Numerical comparison, verification and conclusions}
\subsection{Explicit algorithms versus NumPy}
The differences below are the maximum absolute coefficient differences against \texttt{numpy.linalg.lstsq}. All values refer to the same design and objective. Population rows use the scaled variable $u$; differences are in those fitted coefficients.
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\toprule
Case & $\kappa_2(A)$ & $\kappa_2(A^TA)$ & QR difference & Normal difference\\\midrule
@@CHECK_TABLE@@
\bottomrule\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The explicit methods agree with NumPy to floating-point precision. The agreement of normal-equation solutions does not imply stability for all problems. For the quadratic log-population design, rescaling $t$ to $u=t/100$ reduces $\kappa_2(A)$ from about @@COND_RAW@@ to @@COND_SCALED@@. The Legendre Gram matrix is diagonal, while the monomial Gram matrix couples even terms; both express the same projection principle.

\subsection{Verification and limitations}
All @@CHECK_COUNT@@ numerical and source-data checks passed. Checks include exact rational solutions for the small discrete systems, orthogonality of every projection, independent 160-node Gauss--Legendre quadrature for continuous errors, separate LAPACK reference fits, full-rank designs, endpoint closure, CSV consistency, and exact agreement of the population and airfoil rows with the source PDF. Source-file SHA-256 hashes are recorded before the experiment. The verification record stores each condition individually.

The original equation ambiguity is retained explicitly. Log-space fitting is distinguished from direct population fitting. The population comparison uses the training observations only, and the airfoil fit uses the coordinates supplied with the assignment without asserting aerodynamic performance. No timing comparison is claimed for these small systems.

\subsection{Reproduction and file guide}
Run the following commands from the \texttt{lab3} directory using a Python environment with NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib and PyMuPDF:
\begin{verbatim}
../.venv/bin/python experiment/run_experiment.py
../.venv/bin/python experiment/verify_results.py
../.venv/bin/python experiment/build_report.py
bash report/compile.sh
\end{verbatim}
The execution used NumPy @@NUMPY@@, SciPy @@SCIPY@@ and Matplotlib @@MATPLOTLIB@@. Detailed runtime versions are in \texttt{experiment/results/results.json}. Input CSV files are in \texttt{experiment/data}; numerical tables and separate PDF/PNG plots are in \texttt{experiment/results}. This report's \texttt{main.tex} embeds its vector plots and can be compiled as one standalone file, including in the Codex LaTeX editor.

Least squares is a projection onto a chosen space. A good implementation must specify that space, its data weights, the residual sign and the error scale. In this lab, QR and orthogonality checks connect the theory to reproducible numerical results; basis selection and the fitting objective govern what those results mean.

